.TH POWERPROFILE 1 "September 2026" "powerprofile VERSION" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
powerprofile \- switch between laptop power profiles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B powerprofile
.RB [ \-\-status ]
.RB [ \-\-list ]
.RB [ \-\-auto ]
.RB [ \-\-profile
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-\-help ]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B powerprofile
applies a power profile: a named group of settings read from
.IR /etc/powerprofile.conf .
A profile can set the CPU energy performance preference, turbo and
performance cap, the RAPL power limits, PCIe ASPM, Wi\-Fi power saving, the
embedded controller settings of an MSI laptop (through the
.BR msi\-ec
kernel driver), nvidia\-powerd, and the screen brightness, and can run an
extra command.
See
.BR powerprofile.conf (5).

.PP
The profiles
.B bat
and
.B ac
are chosen automatically from the AC adapter. Any other profile, such as
.B gaming
or
.BR saver ,
is a manual override.

.PP
.B powerprofile
runs as root and never talks to X. The display refresh rate of a profile is
applied by
.BR powerprofile\-x (1),
which follows the profile recorded in
.IR /run/powerprofile\-last .

.PP
With no options,
.B powerprofile
prints its status, equivalent to
.BR \-\-status .

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-status
Print whether the AC adapter is online, the profile applied last, the manual
override if any, the cpufreq driver and governor, and the live CPU and RAPL values.
.B epp: n/a
means this machine has no energy performance preference, which needs HWP
(Intel Speed Shift); the governor and the energy performance bias are the
controls then. Needs no privileges. This is the
default.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print the profile names, one per line, for completion and scripts.
.TP
.B \-\-auto
Apply
.B ac
on AC power and
.B bat
on battery. A manual override is kept while it still fits its
.B only
setting and dropped once it does not, so plugging in ends a
battery\-only override and unplugging ends an AC\-only one.
.TP
.BI \-\-profile " NAME"
Apply the profile NAME now. A profile other than
.B bat
and
.B ac
becomes the manual override. A profile that is limited by
.B only
to the other power source is refused.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
With
.B \-\-auto
or
.BR \-\-profile ,
print what would be done and change nothing. Needs no privileges.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read FILE instead of
.IR /etc/powerprofile.conf .
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Log what is being done to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.

.SH BEHAVIOUR
Brightness is applied only when the profile changes, so applying the same
profile again, for example after resume, does not undo a brightness you set
by hand.

.PP
The firmware RAPL limits are read the first time
.B powerprofile
runs after boot and are restored whenever a profile leaves
.B pl1
and
.B pl2
unset.

.PP
Wi\-Fi power saving is set on every wireless interface, found in sysfs so no
device is named, with
.BR iw (8):
that changes the running device at once, which NetworkManager cannot do, as it
refuses to reapply the setting to an active connection. The same setting is
also put on the interface's NetworkManager connection, in memory only, so that
a later reconnect keeps it. Without NetworkManager only the running device
changes. A connection made after the profile was applied uses NetworkManager's
own default until the profile is applied again.

.PP
Embedded controller values are checked against what the firmware accepts
before anything is written. A value it does not accept, or an attribute the
firmware does not have, is reported and skipped; the rest of the profile is
still applied.

.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/powerprofile.conf
The configuration. As root,
.B powerprofile
refuses a file that is not owned by root or that others can write, because
.B run
executes as root.
.TP
.I /run/powerprofile\-last
The profile applied last.
.TP
.I /run/powerprofile\-override
The active manual override.
.TP
.I /run/powerprofile\-rapl
The firmware RAPL limits captured this boot.

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Apply whichever of bat and ac fits, as the systemd unit and the udev rule do:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo powerprofile \-\-auto
.fi
.RE
.PP
Switch to the gaming profile, then back:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo powerprofile \-\-profile gaming
sudo powerprofile \-\-auto
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what a profile would do without doing it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
powerprofile \-\-dry\-run \-\-profile saver
.fi
.RE

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR powerprofile.conf (5),
.BR powerprofile\-x (1),
.BR msiecctl (1)
